{
 "id": "2021-mitchell-soundscape",
 "name": "Soundscape Survey on Site (ISO 12913)",
 "description": "On-site soundscape questionnaire following ISO/TS 12913-2 Method A, as used by Mitchell et al. (2021, JASA) in 13 public spaces in London and Venice (N = 1,136 questionnaires). People stopped in an urban open space listen for a moment, note the sound sources they hear, rate how much they hear four types of sound (traffic noise, other noise, human sounds, natural sounds; 1 = not at all to 5 = dominates completely) and rate eight perceived affective qualities (pleasant, chaotic, vibrant, uneventful, calm, annoying, eventful, monotonous; 1 = strongly disagree to 5 = strongly agree). The page projects the eight answers onto ISOPleasant and ISOEventful (ISO/TS 12913-3) and shows the participant where the place sits on the soundscape circumplex. The researcher gives each location a code. Analysis as in the paper: per-location means of ISOPleasant / ISOEventful and source dominance; the paper modelled them with linear multilevel models (location as random effect). Differences from the original study: (1) only the questionnaire parts the paper reports (sound source dominance and the eight PAQs, plus the open sound-source note) are included; the full SSID protocol has more items (e.g. overall quality, loudness, demographics) and binaural recordings, which are not part of this template; (2) the PAQ instruction wording follows ISO/TS 12913-2 Method A as summarised in the paper; (3) the ISO coordinates and the paper's exclusion flag (same non-neutral answer to all eight words) are computed on the page and stored as iso_coordinates; the paper computed them afterwards; (4) no sound recordings or listening test.",
 "author": "Mitchell, Oberman, Aletta, Kachlicka, Lionello, Erfanian & Kang",
 "year": "2021",
